  <h2>What goes on a drywall estimate</h2>
  <p>
    A drywall estimate has to answer the questions a homeowner or GC will use to compare you with
    the next bid: how much board, what kind, what finish level and what texture. That is the job of
    the specs strip under the header. Board area, board type, finish level, ceiling height and
    texture are what make two drywall prices comparable. The line items then follow the order the
    work happens:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Hang:</strong> board by type and square foot, soffits and beam wraps by the linear foot,
      and delivery and stocking.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tape and finish:</strong> taping, bedding and finish coats to the stated level, corner bead,
      and window returns.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Texture, specialty and cleanup:</strong> ceiling or wall texture, access panels, floor protection
      and haul-off.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Upgrades the customer might want, such as a Level 5 skim coat or an extra room, go in the
    optional-items table, priced but outside the total.
  </p>

  <h2>How to take off drywall</h2>
  <p>
    Drywall is ordered by the sheet and usually priced by the square foot of board hung. For each
    room, wall area is the wall length times the height, less any large openings, and ceiling area
    is length times width. Soffits and beam wraps are their length times their girth, both sides
    plus the bottom. Add the rooms up, add waste, and divide by the area of the sheet you plan to
    hang:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Sheet</th><th>Sq ft per sheet</th><th>Sheets per 1,000 sq ft, with 10% waste</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>4×8</td><td>32</td><td>35</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4×10</td><td>40</td><td>28</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4×12</td><td>48</td><td>23</td></tr>
      <tr><td>54 in × 12 ft</td><td>54</td><td>21</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The Drywall Takeoff sheet does this room by room and splits the board by type. Rooms marked Y,
    such as baths, go to moisture-resistant board, ceilings to ceiling board, and everything else to
    regular wall board, each with its own sheet length. From the total board area it estimates joint
    compound, paper tape, screws and corner bead sticks, and from your production rates, the hang,
    finish and texture hours. The material rates are common rules of thumb (about 10 gallons of
    ready-mixed compound, 370 feet of tape and 1,000 screws per 1,000 square feet of board), so
    every one is editable. Check the product labels and the fastening schedule.
  </p>

  <h2>Pricing drywall line by line</h2>
  <p>
    Price each board type on its own line. Ceiling board hangs slower than walls, moisture-resistant
    board costs more, and soffits eat hours out of proportion to their square footage. Finishing
    works the same way: corner bead and window returns get their own lines, because a basement full
    of soffits and egress windows takes far longer to finish than the same area of flat wall. Build
    every unit price from cost on the Unit Price Builder: material, plus labor hours at your fully
    loaded rate, plus consumables, then overhead, then profit as a
    <a href="/blog/construction-markup-vs-margin/">margin on price rather than a markup</a>. Board
    rows there are per MSF, 1,000 square feet, so the hours read clearly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The completed example is a basement finish in suburban Kansas City, hung on framing by others:
    3,350 square feet of board, including 274 square feet of moisture-resistant board in the bath
    and 64 feet of duct soffit. Hang comes to $5,240.70, tape and finish to $4,492.50, and texture,
    access panels and cleanup to $1,509.80. That is $11,243.00 in total, or about $3.36 per square
    foot of board. There is no tax line because the contractor pays tax on materials when buying
    them and includes it in the prices. The 30% deposit is $3,372.90, due on acceptance to schedule
    the crew and order board. On the takeoff sheet the same rooms come to 48 wall sheets and 23
    ceiling sheets in 4×12, 10 moisture-resistant sheets in 4×8, and about 90 labor hours.
  </p>

  <h2>Finish levels, and what changes after hang</h2>
  <p>
    The Gypsum Association’s GA-214 defines finish levels 0 through 5. Most painted walls are
    finished to Level 4. Level 5 adds a skim coat over the entire surface for critical lighting:
    raking light from a window, glossy paint, a projector wall. Put the level on the estimate by
    number. “Smooth walls” means one thing to a homeowner and another to a finisher, and a skim coat
    priced as an option keeps that argument off the job.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other source of disputes is what turns up between the estimate and the hang: bowed studs,
    missing blocking, a new soffit for a relocated duct, or an owner who adds a room. The terms in
    the example require rough-in inspections to be signed off before the crew hangs, and bill added
    board at the estimate’s unit prices. Anything else gets written up as a
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a> and signed before the work is done. When the job
    is finished, bill the balance on an <a href="/templates/construction-invoice/">invoice</a> that references
    the estimate number and lists the approved changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Estimate, quote or proposal?</h2>
  <p>
    For a basement, a remodel or a small addition, this customer-facing estimate is usually the
    whole sale: a price, the terms and a signature. On a commercial job bid to a GC from drawings
    and a spec, where the architect sets the wall types, rated assemblies and finish levels, build
    your cost on the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate worksheet</a> and send a
    <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">bid proposal</a> with inclusions, exclusions and alternates.
    For a small, well-defined job such as a garage or a patch-and-texture repair, a
    <a href="/templates/construction-quote/">construction quote</a> with one firm price is enough.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes on drywall estimates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      One square-foot price for everything. Soffits, bead and returns are the slow work, and burying
      them loses money on every basement.
    </li>
    <li>
      No finish level. Without a number on the estimate, a customer who expected Level 5 ends up
      looking at Level 4 walls under a window.
    </li>
    <li>
      Showing the customer ordered sheets instead of measured board. Waste belongs in your unit
      price, not in their quantity.
    </li>
    <li>Ordering 12-foot sheets for a basement without checking the stairs.</li>
    <li>
      No valid-until date. Board prices move, and an open-ended estimate can be held to old ones.
    </li>
  </ul>
